115	+## Signature & trust model
116	+
117	+Two independent authenticity layers, per Fusor's adapter contract
118	+(`client-adapters.md` §Signatures & security):
119	+
120	+- **Outer — the delivery is genuinely from Fusor.** Streaming transport:
121	+  the authenticated WebSocket session. Webhook transport: Fusor's
122	+  `x-spectrum-signature` / `x-spectrum-timestamp` HMAC, verified by the SDK
123	+  when `Spectrum({ webhookSecret })` is set. Not this contract's concern.
124	+- **Inner — the envelope is genuinely from an iMessage publisher.** The
125	+  publisher signs the synthetic request:
126	+
127	+  ```
128	+  x-imessage-timestamp: <unix seconds at signing>
129	+  x-imessage-signature: hex(HMAC-SHA256(secret, "{timestamp}.{body}"))
130	+  ```
131	+
132	+  `body` is the exact JSON bytes after the blank line. The consumer
133	+  (`verify.ts`) checks this only when the provider config sets
134	+  `webhookSigningSecret`: it requires both headers, rejects timestamps
135	+  outside `replayToleranceSec` (default 300s) of its clock, and compares
136	+  the HMAC with a constant-time equality. Any failure throws — Fusor
137	+  treats the event as poison (no retry).
138	+
139	+  **Unsigned envelopes are accepted only while no secret is configured.**
140	+  Until publishers sign, authenticity rests entirely on the outer layer —
141	+  webhook-transport consumers should set `webhookSecret`. Once a publisher
142	+  rolls out signing, configure the shared secret on both ends; from then
143	+  on every delivery must carry a fresh, valid signature.
144	+
